[quote="arenson9"]

Having Jim&#39;s Hat and a Sudden Contract in Chrono Ranger&#39;s starting hand were a big part of our success.

[/quote]

&nbsp;

I happen to remember that by Chrono Ranger&#39;s third turn, By Any Means and Ultimate Target were both on Dawn. Chrono Ranger played two different cards that dealt 1 damage each and then used the Compound Bow to do deal 1 damage two more times. Each of those four instances of dealing damage were increased by two for a total of 12 damage.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

That only leaves 68 other points and we had 11 other hero turns in which to do damage. The Kraken did the last three points, so that&#39;s 65 points of damage over 11 hero turns for an average of just shy of six points per hero turn. Factor in that the Bounty which increases all damage by one (Ultimate Target?) was on Dawn by the second hero turn, boosting every attack. On Haka&#39;s first turn, for instance, an Elbow Smash and his base power did a total of seven points of damage. Also factor in that with the other Bounty, Chrono Ranger got a power use the first time each turn that Dawn dealt damage. Only got to take advantage of that once, but every little bit counts.

&nbsp;

Ah! I also just remembered that Dawn had &nbsp;an Ongoing which caused her to hit herself. &nbsp;I think she took that damage twice.
<p style="font-size: 12.727272033691406px; line-height: 20px;">&nbsp;
<p style="font-size: 12.727272033691406px; line-height: 20px;"><span style="font-size: 12.727272033691406px; line-height: 20px;">Yes, the Vengeance heroes dished out a lot of damage, but not so amazingly well as to prove that they are overpowered. 
